SUMMARY:Clean-Life Living
DESCRIPTION:From Hip-Hop to Holistic Health: Clean-Life LIVING Brings Fresh Perspectives on Living Well to Montclair \nDarryl “DMC” McDaniels\, Dr. Brian Clement\, Warren Zanes and other notable guests come together October 24 for an afternoon of inspiration\, connection and community benefiting Montclair Helps. \nMONTCLAIR\, NJ — What do a hip-hop legend\, a holistic wellness pioneer\, a rock musician and bestselling author\, a community builder and a scholar of American transcendentalism have in common? \nMore than you might think. \nOn Saturday\, October 24\, Montclair will host Clean-Life LIVING\, an afternoon designed to explore what it means to live well in an increasingly complicated\, demanding and often disconnected world. \nTaking place from 3:30 to 6:30 p.m. at Union Congregational Church\, the event brings together an eclectic group of speakers whose experiences span music\, nutrition\, psychology\, creativity\, philosophy and community building. \nThe theme is simple: Unplug. Reset. Integrate. \nAnd the underlying idea is that living well isn’t about finding one perfect diet\, adopting a single wellness practice or following someone else’s prescription for a better life. It’s about taking a broader look at how we live—and becoming more intentional about integrating the things that help us flourish. \n“Clean-Life LIVING is about more than what we eat or the products we use\,” explains the event’s organizing philosophy. “It’s about removing what gets in the way of feeling and living well—and making room for the practices that sustain us.” \nThe afternoon will benefit Montclair Helps\, a local nonprofit providing emergency financial assistance to neighbors facing unexpected crises. \nAn Unlikely Gathering with a Common Purpose \nAmong the featured guests is Darryl “DMC” McDaniels\, co-founder of the groundbreaking hip-hop group Run-DMC. \nWhile McDaniels helped transform popular music and culture\, his story extends far beyond the stage. His personal experiences with mental health challenges\, self-discovery and his advocacy for children in foster care have made him an influential voice on resilience\, belonging and purpose. \nIn a conversation centered on Finding Your Voice & Your Purpose\, McDaniels will share perspectives on navigating adversity\, discovering authenticity and finding meaning through self-expression\, human connection and giving back. \nDr. Brian Clement\, co-director of Hippocrates Wellness\, brings a very different perspective. For more than five decades\, Clement has advocated for plant-based nutrition\, raw and living foods\, and lifestyle-centered approaches to well-being. \nHis presentation\, Food as Medicine\, will explore the relationship between what we eat\, the habits we cultivate and our overall approach to health. Clement’s perspective invites attendees to reconsider the role that everyday nutritional choices can play in supporting well-being. \nWarren Zanes brings yet another dimension to the conversation: the power of creativity and the stories we tell about ourselves. \nA former guitarist with the rock band Del Fuegos\, Zanes is also the bestselling author of Petty: The Biography and Deliver Me from Nowhere\, his acclaimed exploration of Bruce Springsteen’s Nebraska album\, which inspired the feature film about its creation. \nDrawing on his experiences as a musician\, writer and storyteller\, Zanes will explore creativity\, generational trauma and the ways our personal narratives can either limit or expand our sense of possibility. \nHis presentation\, A Creative Life & Integrated Living\, asks a compelling question: How might our lives change if we became more conscious of the stories that shape our identities? \nThe Importance of Connection—and the Courage to Be Ourselves \nFor Anjali Dalal\, founder of Salonette\, well-being is inseparable from human connection. \nA Yale Law School graduate and former attorney\, Dalal began bringing people together through intimate gatherings in her own living room. Those experiences eventually inspired Salonette\, an initiative centered on meaningful conversation\, curiosity and community. \nIn a conversation with Emily Kemeny-Rose\, Dalal will explore how participation\, openness and authentic relationships contribute to our sense of belonging—and why strengthening our connections may be one of the most important investments we can make in ourselves. \nThe afternoon will also feature Barbara Solowey\, a scholar of American transcendentalism and longtime educator who has spent decades teaching the works of Ralph Waldo Emerson\, Henry David Thoreau\, Walt Whitman and other influential American writers. \nDrawing on Emerson’s celebrated essay Self-Reliance\, Solowey will consider the relevance of his ideas in contemporary life. \nWhat does it mean to trust our own judgment? How do we resist the pressure to conform? And how can we cultivate the courage to live more authentically? \nHer presentation\, Emerson’s Self-Reliance in Everyday Life\, will bring these timeless philosophical questions into the practical realities of modern living. \nKeeping the afternoon lively will be Craig Baldo\, a Montclair comedian whose credits include Late Night with Conan O’Brien\, Comedy Central and audience warm-up work for Last Week Tonight with John Oliver. Baldo will serve as host and master of ceremonies\, bringing humor and energy to an afternoon that promises both reflection and entertainment. \nMore Than Inspiration: Something to Take Home \nAlthough the speakers come from remarkably different backgrounds\, the event’s organizers have invited each of them to work within a shared framework. \nEach will reflect on the experiences that shaped their perspective\, connect their particular area of expertise to the broader picture of living well\, and share insights that others might apply to their own lives. \nImportantly\, speakers have also been encouraged to conclude with a practical invitation—a specific action\, activity or practice that attendees can explore for themselves. \nThe objective is not simply to leave people feeling inspired\, but to help them translate that inspiration into everyday life. \nThe idea of integration is central to the event. \nNutrition\, movement\, sleep\, emotional well-being\, creativity\, spirituality and human connection are often treated as separate pursuits. Clean-Life LIVING encourages attendees to consider how these dimensions of life intersect and reinforce one another. \nSometimes living better means introducing a new practice. Other times it means recognizing something we’ve neglected\, reconsidering an old assumption or simply becoming more conscious of what already matters to us. \nThe organizers hope attendees leave with an expanded perspective\, a renewed sense of possibility and at least one manageable step toward living more intentionally. \nLiving Well. Helping Neighbors. \nBeyond the speakers and conversations\, Clean-Life LIVING has another important purpose: supporting neighbors in need. \nThe event is a fundraiser for Montclair Helps\, a community nonprofit that provides direct financial assistance to individuals and families facing unexpected emergencies. \nWorking through local nonprofit partners\, Montclair Helps responds to urgent needs within 12 to 48 hours\, providing one-time grants ranging from $200 to $2\,000. \nThe organization has already distributed nearly $300\,000 in direct assistance\, helping local residents navigate difficult moments when relatively modest financial support can make a meaningful difference. \nIn that sense\, the event’s message extends beyond individual wellness. \nTaking better care of ourselves\, the organizers suggest\, is connected to how we care for one another—and ultimately to the health and vitality of the community we share. \nIt’s a fitting message for an event that brings together so many different voices under one roof. \nAnd perhaps that’s the larger point of Clean-Life LIVING: There is no single formula for living well. But there is much we can learn from one another. \nEvent Information \nClean-Life LIVING — Unplug. Reset. Integrate. \nDate: Saturday\, October 24\, 2026\nTime: 3:30–6:30 p.m.\nLocation: Union Congregational Church\, Montclair\, NJ \nFeatured guests: Darryl “DMC” McDaniels\, Dr. Brian Clement\, Warren Zanes\, Anjali Dalal and Barbara Solowey. Hosted by Craig Baldo. \nTickets: $45 in advance; $55 at the door. \nBeneficiary: Montclair Helps. All event donations received benefit people facing crises.\nTickets: https://www.zeffy.com/en-US/ticketing/clean-life-living\nMore information: https://www.montclairhelps.org/\nAttendees are encouraged to submit questions for the speakers in advance. \nShare this:\n				Share on Facebook (Opens in new window)\n				Facebook\n			\n				Share on Bluesky (Opens in new window)\n				Bluesky\n			\n				Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window)\n				LinkedIn\n			\n				Share on Threads (Opens in new window)\n				Threads\n			\n				Share on Nextdoor (Opens in new window)\n				Nextdoor\n			\n				Share on WhatsApp (Opens in new window)\n				WhatsApp\n			\n				Email a link to a friend (Opens in new window)\n				Email
